A||||11||Gold-86SE||84 keeps burning the horn fuse||||8||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||12-29-2001||04:19 PM||||<BR>I am really becoming frustrated with the fuse block arrangement on this 84. The horn is routed with the power mirrors, and lighter.  Everytime I replace a blown 20amp fuse, it blows again.<P>I don't even get a chance to use the horn, the fuse blows as soon as I place it in the fuse box. It appears that I am drawing way too much current and sending the fuse into a tizzy.<P>I did a search and found one thread that was relevant <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/011559.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/011559.html</A>  and looked in ogre's cave but neither helped me resolve the problem.<P>Let me add this tidbit.  The turn signal switch when idicating a right hand turn will not automatically turn off once I have completed my turn, I have to manually reach out and push down the turn signal stalk.  I am curious if this has given me a broken wire or something in the steering column, and related to my fuse problem.<P>What is going on? I am more than frustrated.<BR>||||reg||8
Z||000001||Mark||12-29-2001||04:51 PM||||Gold....<P>Sounds like you have a solid short circuit,which in many ways is alot better than an intermittant short circuit.<P>First, I would suggest you temporarily "replace" the fuse that keeps blowing with an ordinary automotive (12 volt) light bulb, like a tail lamp or turn signal or such.  The fault will cause this bulb to illuminate.  Note that the resistance of the bulb filament will limit the current through the circuit to only a few amperes.  If the bulb glows brightly, you will know that you have a "good", low resistance short.  If this is the case, then simply go around and disconnect all of the other loads on that same circuit (e.g., horn, horn relay, lighter, etc.), one at a time.  When the test bulb goes out, you found the problem circuit.  Then check the wiring, sockets, etc. of the suspect circuit for faults. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||12-29-2001||04:54 PM||||It sounds like you've got problems in the column..... If something in there has broken and gotten into the horn ring then yes, they could be related. Either way your turn signal problem will require you to pull the steering wheel and lock plate.<P>The light bulb is a good tester.... <P>One thing to try.... disconnect the harness coming down from the steering wheel... If it's anything in the column causing the short then that will tell you. You may have to remove some trim to get at the harness but you won't need to mess with the wheel and lock plate right away.....<P>Some parts for the turn signal switch are available thru MotorMite's product lines. The red carded HELP! stuff I think. take the bad parts with you to match them up...<P>Next item is pull the ligher and disconnect it... those sockets go bad and can short. It's often hard to tell just looking at them.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gold-86SE||12-29-2001||05:00 PM||||<BR>ogre, tried your idea because it was the fastest test, pulled the two wiring connectors apart the come out of the steering column, put a fuse in and still blows the 20amp fuse.<P>additional info<BR>I don't have a stereo in the fiero, and i have the lighter disconnected.  What else should I check.<P>Time to try mark's idea...<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 12-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||12-29-2001||05:05 PM||||was editing while you was testing.... oh well... hook up the light as mark said but disconnect the lighter next....||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||12-29-2001||05:09 PM||||Stop That  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I don't have wiring diagrams specific to 84...<P>The power mirrors are on it? take the plug off the switch for them.... ||||reg||10
Z||000006||Gold-86SE||12-29-2001||05:35 PM||||<BR>ok guys.<P>first, glad you are here so i can bounce this stuff off you.<P>second, here is what is currently disconnected:<P>1) courtecy center (horn relay, chime box, etc.)<BR>2) steering column wiring connectors<BR>3) floor lights<BR>4) dome lights<BR>5) ciggy lighter<BR>6) no radio in the fiero<BR>7) power mirrors (both doors)<BR>8) power locks (both doors)<BR>9) both horns<P>and i am still running extremely high on amps, i still blow a 20 amp fuse.<P>what gives?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 12-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula88||12-29-2001||05:46 PM||||On my Formula, my cigar lighter was all corroded up and it was blowing the fuse - but not immediately like yours.  Go down the list of everything on that circuit and disconnect all of it.  Put a new fuse in, then reconnect the items one at a time till it blows.  If it blows with all the loads disconnected, you've got a pinched or shorted wire in the wiring harness.||||reg||6
Z||000008||Gold-86SE||12-29-2001||06:19 PM||||<BR>Formula88, I am afraid that you have me nailed to rights on this<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>you've got a pinched or shorted wire in the wiring harness.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The thing is exactly where is it? And how do I find it?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||12-30-2001||03:10 PM||||Just to double check... You have the ligher socket center contact physically disconnected from the harness...<P>Where did you disconnect the mirrors? Pull the mirror switch. that will separate the complete door mirror harness from the circuit.<P>Even tho there is no radio,,, find the connectors and follow the wires to make sure they've never had any splices etc. You're after the small 2 wire connector that provides the radio memory power.<P>There aren't that many thing on that fuse I don't think..... <P>At the very worst, I'd say pull the dash and console and start physical inspection of things. If someone made modification to the harness for a cell phone or other device then there may be wires spliced and shoved in almost anyplace. They could now be contacting the frame under the dash and shorting out.<P>The dash isn't that hard to take out, just annoying. It's held by 4 screws under the speakers and 2 bolts on the bottom outboard corners. Watch for 2 plastic tabe where the top of the console frame hooks to the center of the dash. The console will flex enough to let them go it your careful.<P>My car had a cell phone in it years ago... I still haven't eliminated all the wires from that. Those old phones used allot of wires. not like today's jobs that usually just plug into the lighter.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Gold-86SE||12-30-2001||11:38 PM||||<BR>UPDATE!<P><I><B>FOUND THE PROBLEM</B></I><P>I don't like shade tree mechanics... too bad I am the shade tree mechanic that made my problem.<P>First, let me illuminate (pun intended... read on) on the horn relay 20 amp fuse for an 84. It is on the circuit which contains the following items:<P>1) lighter<BR>2) clock memory<BR>3) power mirrors<BR>4) power locks<BR>5) horn relay<BR>6) dome lights<BR>7) floor lights<BR>8) <B>TRUNK</B> light<P>I bold capitalize the trunk light, because it was the source of my problem.  No where in my fuse break down did it mention the trunk light as being on this circuit with the horn relay. Nor was I smart enough to figure that the trunk light is consider part of the courtesy lights.  Besides, I rarely see the trunk light anyway, so it does not occur to me to be a problem.<P>Unfortunately, I pulled the console, the radio, the steering column, the dash, and disconnected the front wiring harness (by the brake booster).  After doing all of this I happen to read a different section of one of my manuals and like a footnote it mentions the trunk light as being on the same circuit as the horn relay.  So, I thought I have tried everything else, let me try that... and that is where my problem was.<P>The light cover in the trunk has a side clip that holds the light in place.  Some time during the last two years, I broke that clip and removed the light and pushed the fixture back into the hole.  Creating my problem... shorting out the circuit, because the light fixture was touching the frame.<P>As a footnote I would like to add one thing. Something that I did not see diagramed in the electrical section of any manual.  It is a control circuit (of some type) for this 20 amp horn relay.<P>The location: front driver side under the dash.  Follow the hood release cable to where it goes through the wall, on the left side of that cable you should see a white plastic circuit board with several wires plugged in to it.  From what I can tell, you can disconnect all items contained on the 20 amp horn relay fuse at this location.  But be mindful it is difficult to reach.<P><BR><I><B>Thanks guys</B></I> for the help and theogre, unfortunately I had to do the worst.  Best I guess, I was able to do a good cleaning on the dash and console while they were out.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||12-30-2001||11:48 PM||||bloody hell. the trunk light.... not having a trunk light, I usually forget those even exist... rats. Glad you finally found it.||||reg||7
